  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Texas had $87.9B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date, with 175,428 awards and an average award size of $501K. Texas obligations were up 1.8% year over year. The Department of Defense obligated $39.2B and the Department of Homeland Security obligated $25.5B, together accounting for the largest share of federal buying in the state. Federal obligations to major vendors included LOCKHEED MARTIN CORPORATION at $12.6B and MCKESSON CORPORATION at $12.2B, followed by a second LOCKHEED MARTIN CORPORATION entry at $9.5B. Commercial and Institutional Building Construction led industry spending at $17.6B. Contracting activity shows growth relative to recent years, following FY25 obligations of $86.4B and a FY24 trough of $59.3B. All figures are contract obligations and current-year totals are partial, fiscal year to date.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What drove federal contracting totals in Texas?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense obligated $39.2B and the Department of Homeland Security obligated $25.5B, making those two agencies the top buyers in Texas. The Department of Veterans Affairs obligated $17.5B, which further concentrated spending among the top three agencies. FY2026 to date obligations of $87.9B follow FY25 obligations of $86.4B and FY24 obligations of $59.3B, showing month-to-date totals above the prior fiscal year. Total award count was 175,428 with an average award size of $501K."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which industries and vendors dominate federal spending in Texas?",
        "body": "Commercial and Institutional Building Construction received $17.6B, making it the largest industry by obligations. Aircraft Manufacturing received $12.3B and Pharmaceutical Preparation Manufacturing received $12.2B, while Guided Missile and Space Vehicle Manufacturing received $11.8B. LOCKHEED MARTIN CORPORATION received $12.6B and MCKESSON CORPORATION received $12.2B, with another LOCKHEED MARTIN CORPORATION entry at $9.5B among the top vendors."
      }
    ]
  },
